# Web Frontend & Deployment Implementation Plan

We will convert our existing CLI-based GraphRAG agent into a full-stack web application. We will build a modern Next.js frontend interface, a FastAPI backend to serve our agent, and set up a deployment pipeline.

## Goal
To build a stunning, premium Next.js frontend for our conversational AI and deploy both the backend and frontend to the web so it is publicly accessible.

## User Review Required

> [!WARNING]
> **Data Persistence:** The current RAG system relies on a local ChromaDB (`data/chroma_db`) and a local graph pickle file (`data/metakgp_graph.gpickle`). When we deploy, the easiest method is to include this `data/` directory directly in our backend deployment as read-only data. This means the knowledge base is static until re-deployed. If we want it to be dynamically updated in the cloud, we'd need a managed vector DB (like Pinecone) and a managed graph database (like Neo4j Aura). For this plan, I propose we stick to the **read-only bundled data** approach for simplicity.

## Open Questions

> [!IMPORTANT]
> 1. **Next.js + Python Separation:** While Next.js supports Python API routes, libraries like `chromadb` (which has native C++ bindings for `hnswlib`) and `networkx` often exceed serverless function limits and cause build issues on Vercel. Therefore, I propose keeping the Next.js app purely as the frontend on **Vercel**, and hosting the Python backend separately on **Render**. Does this sound good to you?
> 2. **Styling Approach:** Our guidelines recommend Vanilla CSS for complete flexibility and a highly custom aesthetic, but since we are using Next.js, Tailwind CSS is the default and can be very fast to iterate with. Should I proceed with purely custom Vanilla CSS, or use Tailwind CSS to ensure a very premium, tailored look?

